title: Powers, bits, big numbers, and kinds for any kind
card: Chapter 12 | Powers and bits | More ways to work with numbers
say:
  Numbers can do more than add and multiply. Here are powers, the bits inside whole numbers, and numbers too big for any computer's own numbers.
uncard

new: numbers.stella
type fast:
  use: big numbers

  genesis:
    scrawl "2 to the power 10 is ", 2 to the power 10
    scrawl "3 times 2 to the power 3 is ", 3 times 2 to the power 3
    scrawl "12 bitwise and 10 is ", 12 bitwise and 10
    scrawl "12 bitwise or 3 is ", 12 bitwise or 3
    scrawl "1 shifted left by 4 is ", 1 shifted left by 4
    scrawl "2 to the power 100 is ", big power with ("2", 100)
    scrawl "and times itself: ", big times with (big power with ("2", 100), big power with ("2", 100))
  and the universe said i love you
zoom: lines 4-5
spot: line 4
say: To the power multiplies a number by itself: two to the power ten is a thousand and twenty four.
spot: line 5
say:
  A power is worked out first, as in maths. So three times two to the power three is three times eight.
spot: lines 6-8
zoom: lines 6-8
say:
  Every whole number is made of bits, ones and zeros. Bitwise and keeps the bits both numbers have, bitwise or keeps the bits either has, and shifted left moves them along, doubling the number each time.
  Bits are a small, fast way to keep many yes or no answers in one number.
spot: lines 9-10
zoom: lines 9-10
say:
  Numbers have a largest size. The big numbers library has no limit: its numbers are kept as text, as long as they need to be.
spot: off
zoom: out
run
zoom: output
say: Two to the power one hundred has thirty one digits, and times itself, sixty one. Every digit is exact.
wait
zoom: out

card: Chapter 12 | Kinds for any kind | Describing something once
say:
  Some kinds of things work the same way whatever they hold. A stack keeps things on top of each other, whether they're numbers, words or pets.
  Such a kind can be described once, with anything standing in for what it holds.
uncard

new: stacks.stella
type fast:
  a stack of anything has:
    items (a list of anything)
  and the universe said i love you

  to push, for a stack of anything, given x (anything):
    add x to items
  and the universe said i love you

  to top, for a stack of anything, returning anything:
    return last item of items
  and the universe said i love you

  genesis:
    conjure a stack of numbers as counts
    push with (counts, 3)
    push with (counts, 8)
    conjure a stack of text as words
    push with (words, "hello")
    scrawl top counts, " and ", top words
  and the universe said i love you
zoom: lines 1-3
spot: lines 1-2
say: A stack of anything has a list of anything. Anything stands for whatever kind the stack will hold.
zoom: lines 5-11
spot: lines 5-7
say: Push, for a stack of anything, is given anything, and adds it to the stack's items.
spot: lines 9-11
say: Top gives back the last item: again, of whatever kind it is.
zoom: lines 13-19
spot: lines 14-16
say:
  Then a stack of numbers is a kind of its own: the description written out again with anything as a number.
spot: lines 17-18
say: And a stack of text is another. Push and top work on both, and Stellanova still checks you only put numbers on the stack of numbers.
spot: off
zoom: out
run
zoom: output
wait
zoom: out

card: Chapter 12 | Commands for a kind, kept | Doing it later
say:
  In chapter six, a command could be kept as a value. Commands for a kind of thing can be kept the same way.
uncard
new: grow.stella
type fast:
  a pet has:
    name (text)
    age (a number)
  and the universe said i love you

  to grow, for a pet, given years (a number):
    set age to age plus years
  and the universe said i love you

  genesis:
    conjure a pet as rex
    set the name of rex to "Rex"
    set older to the command grow
    set rex to invoke older with (rex, 2)
    scrawl rex
  and the universe said i love you
zoom: lines 10-16
spot: line 13
say: The command grow is kept in a name. It's a command given a pet and a number, returning a pet.
spot: line 14
say: Invoke gives it rex and two. It changes a copy of rex, and gives the changed copy back.
spot: off
zoom: out
run
zoom: output
wait
zoom: out
